<Doc
  title="Getting started"
  description="Two ways in: a machine of your own, or Exeora Cloud. Both end with a URL to point a client at."
  canonicalPath="/docs/"
>
  <p>
    Exeora connects an AI client to a project: a repository with a working copy on a machine, where
    an agent can read, edit and run things. There are two ways to give a project a machine, and
    neither is the lesser one. A machine of your own keeps the code where it already is. Exeora
    Cloud clones the repository onto an instance, a machine Exeora runs, so an agent can work with
    nothing of yours switched on. A project can use both, and you can add the second one later.
  </p>

  <h2 id="your-machine">A machine of your own</h2>

  <InstallCommands id="docs-getting-started" />

  <pre>exeora connect
exeora project add the-project-you-want-to-serve</pre>

  <p>
    That is the whole setup. <code>connect</code> opens a browser to sign in if you are not signed
    in and registers the machine if it is not registered. It does not have to run inside a project
    directory. <code>exeora project add</code> registers a directory to serve, where it is. Each
    step is skipped when it is already done, so running <code>connect</code> again just connects.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nothing is uploaded, no port is opened, and no tunnel is configured, because the CLI dials out
    to the gateway and holds that connection open. Nothing ever dials in, which is why the same
    command works on a laptop behind NAT and on a box behind a corporate firewall.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>exeora project add</code> also takes a repository instead of a directory:{" "}
    <code>exeora project add owner/repo</code> clones it into this machine's projects folder,{" "}
    <code>~/exeora</code> unless you chose another, and registers the clone. The first time{" "}
    <code>connect</code> runs at a terminal it asks where that folder should be.
  </p>

  <p>
    It prints an MCP URL. <a href="/docs/clients/">Point a client at it</a>, and leave{" "}
    <code>connect</code> running: nothing on this machine is served while it is not.
  </p>

  <p>
    The installer selects the release for your operating system and CPU, verifies its SHA-256
    checksum, and puts <code>exeora</code> on your PATH. The Linux binary needs glibc 2.31 or
    newer: Ubuntu 20.04 LTS, 22.04, 24.04, Debian 11 and later, current Fedora releases, and RHEL 9
    and later.
  </p>

  <p>
    Later, run <code>exeora upgrade</code>. It downloads the newest release for your platform,
    verifies the published checksum, and securely replaces the current binary.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cloud">Exeora Cloud</h2>

  <p>
    This way starts in the dashboard and needs no CLI. Exeora Cloud is enabled per account by an
    administrator while there is no billing, so an account that does not have it yet is told so
    where the choice is offered.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      First, sign in to the dashboard and open <strong>Settings</strong>. Under{" "}
      <strong>GitHub</strong>, choose <strong>Connect GitHub</strong> and pick, on GitHub, which
      repositories Exeora is given. See <a href="/docs/github/">connecting GitHub</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Then open <strong>Projects</strong> and choose <strong>Add project</strong>. Pick the
      repository from the list. There is no address to type and no token to paste.
    </li>
    <li>
      Last, choose Exeora Cloud as the place it lives first. Exeora clones the repository onto an
      instance and connects the CLI there.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The project's page shows its MCP URL as soon as the project exists, and the state of the
    instance until it is ready. A repository on another Git server is added from the same dialog,
    with its https address and, when it is private, a token. See{" "}
    <a href="/docs/cloud/">Exeora Cloud</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What a project is</h2>

  <p>
    A project is a repository. It lives in one or more locations: your machines and Exeora Cloud.
    One location is the default, and a call that names no workspace lands there. A workspace is a
    branch with a working copy of its own, in one location. See{" "}
    <a href="/docs/projects/">projects, locations and workspaces</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The boundary every tool is confined to is the project's directory in the location the call
    lands on: the project root, or the working copy of the workspace the call names. An agent
    cannot read, write or run anything outside it through a path: paths are resolved and checked
    against that directory before anything touches the disk.
  </p>

  <p>
    The CLI refuses to register your home directory or the filesystem root, because either would
    make that boundary the whole machine.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each project gets its own URL and its own token. A token minted for one project is not accepted
    at another, so "this client may work on that repository" is a sentence you can say one
    repository at a time. It covers the project wherever it lives: every location, and every
    workspace in them.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is also one URL that covers several, <code>https://exeora.dev/mcp</code>, so a client can
    be configured once instead of once per project. You choose which projects it may reach when you
    authorize it, and it names the project on each call. See{" "}
    <a href="/docs/clients/#which-url">which URL to use</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What it can do to a machine</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>A new project allows everything.</strong> Until you narrow it, an agent connected to a
    project can read, edit and write any file inside that directory and run any command there. That
    is worth knowing before you connect the first one.
  </p>

  <p>
    From the dashboard you can set a project to read only, name the commands it may run, refuse
    specific commands in any mode, choose which tools exist at all, and ask to confirm every change
    before it happens. See <a href="/docs/policy/">what a project allows</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Whatever you decide, revoking a machine from the dashboard cuts its connection immediately rather
    than at the end of whatever it was doing.
  </p>
</Doc>
